# Catalog NUL-names investigation (OPEN)

There is an open, characterized-but-unsolved bug: the n1 production capture has
directory-name components in the on-disk catalog whose bytes are zeroed while
`name_len` stays correct (38,464 entries in 75 of 264 shards; 16,633 of those in
`uid_shard_362.bin`; 99.63% jemalloc 48-byte class, sharp onset at nlen=32;
sibling-clustered; mixed leftovers are 16-byte-lane partial wipes), plus
duplicate directory entries (e.g. four `data1` dirs under pid=1). Leading
hypothesis: a 16/32/48-byte zeroing event hitting jemalloc 48-byte size-class
slots (name buffers + hash-table entries), breaking HT chains. TSan on a 1.2 B
entry crawl found no production writer race (one tripwire-only getenv race).

`--subtree` now unions every path-equal catalog dir_id (query-side duplicate
tolerance has landed); writer-side NUL / hash-table corruption is still open.

Before changing catalog creation/writing/reading code, or starting any related
debugging or repro work, read `docs/history/catalog-nul-names-investigation.md` — it records
the established facts (do not re-derive them), what was already ruled out (reader
exonerated; ASan/jemalloc synthetic repros all clean), the fixes already landed, the
`ECRAWL_CAT_TRIPWIRE` instrumentation, and the ordered next steps.

If you learn something new about this bug, update that document in the same change.
